Patch 09 -- the hot page is per thread.

`hotpage` and `hotpage_cnt` were process-wide, so every thread running under the
dynarec shared one page marker and one budget. With more than one guest thread
generating code -- which this game has -- thread B's SetHotPage() silently
retargets the marker thread A is still spending its budget on, and neither
thread's self-modifying page keeps the relaxed protection the mechanism exists
to give it. Making both variables `__thread` gives each thread its own marker and
its own budget; nothing else in the mechanism changes.

Cost: PT_TLS memsz grows by 16 bytes per thread.

WHY THIS PATCH EXISTS SEPARATELY. It was found on 2026-08-19 in the box86 source
tree with no patch recording it, while `box86-patches/08` recorded only the
budget hunk -- so the patch series did not reproduce the deployed production
binary. Which binary has which is checkable without an ARM disassembler:

```text
box86-island31            .tbss 0x294   neither hunk
box86-island32-hotpage    .tbss 0x294   patch 08 only
box86-island32-prod       .tbss 0x2a4   patch 08 + this patch   <- production
```

NOT MEASURED, in either direction, on frame rate or on the open Box86 sync-arena
freeze. It is deployed because `launch-play.sh` selects `box86-island32-prod`;
that selection is not carried by a number. `box86-island31` + the FINALPLAY6
allow-list is the measured rollback.

--- a/src/custommem.c
+++ b/src/custommem.c
@@ -881,8 +881,8 @@
     return 1;
 }
 
-uintptr_t hotpage = 0;
-int hotpage_cnt = 0;
+__thread uintptr_t hotpage = 0;
+__thread int hotpage_cnt = 0;
 #define HOTPAGE_MARK 64
 void SetHotPage(uintptr_t addr)
 {
